   "title": "Government working to make infrastructure accessible for differently-abled: Delhi CM",
   "url": "https://www.thehindu.com/news/cities/Delhi/government-working-to-make-infrastructure-accessible-for-differently-abled-delhi-cm/article71646509.ece",
   "domain": "thehindu.com",
   "source": "thehindu.com",
   "published": "Sat, 31 Oct 2026 10:50:21 +0530",
   "_dt": 1793424021.0,
   "ageDays": 0.03,
   "summary": "The CM said that the Delhi government is continuously working to make the capital’s infrastructure fully accessible, ensuring that differently-abled persons can lead a life of dignity and enjoy equal opportunities. The Diwali Bazaar, organised by the Blind Relief Association, has been an important part of Delhi’s Diwali tradition for more than 45 years.",
   "summary_zh": "首席部长表示，德里政府正持续努力，使首都的基础设施完全无障碍，确保残障人士能够有尊严地生活并享有平等机会。由盲人救济协会组织的排灯节集市已成为德里排灯节传统的重要组成部分，已有45多年历史。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription Chief Minister Rekha Gupta on Friday (October 30, 2026) inaugurated the popular ‘Diwali Bazaar 2026’ at the Blind Relief Association premises and visited various stalls and appreciated the handmade products crafted by visually impaired individuals, encouraging them to showcase their skills. The CM said that the Delhi government is continuously working to make the capital’s infrastructure fully accessible, ensuring that differently-abled persons can lead a life of dignity and enjoy equal opportunities. The Diwali Bazaar, organised by the Blind Relief Association, has been an important part of Delhi’s Diwali tradition for more than 45 years. This year, the seven-day bazaar is being held from October 30 to November 5, with more than 250 stalls, the CMO said in a statement. The Chief Minister also visited ‘Cafe Mast’, run by visually challenged youth associated with the organisation, who independently handle all responsibilities, from preparing food to customer service and transactions. Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
